Package: luci-app-homeproxy
Version: 1.0.0-r31
Depends: libc, lua, luci-base, luci-compat, luci-lib-jsonc, libuci-lua, ucode-mod-lua, sing-box, iptables, ip6tables, kmod-nft-tproxy, kmod-ipt-tproxy, kmod-nft-compat, ip-full, curl, ca-bundle
Source: homeproxy-2410
SourceName: luci-app-homeproxy
License: GPL-2.0
Section: luci
Architecture: aarch64_cortex-a53
Installed-Size: 863438
Description: 走进科学 (HomeProxy, ImmortalWrt 24.10 / firewall4 targets)
 A sing-box based transparent proxy, rewritten natively in Lua for the
 ImmortalWrt 24.10 generation: Lua CBI frontend, Lua configuration
 generator, and iptables TPROXY rules installed into the nft ruleset.
 .
 The upstream package needs the JavaScript LuCI client and ucode; this one
 runs against luci-compat's Lua dispatcher and needs only the sing-box
 binary, which it depends on rather than re-shipping. The original r23 build
 bundled its own copy of the binary and declared `Replaces: sing-box`, which
 had two packages owning /usr/bin/sing-box at once -- removing either one
 then deleted the engine the other was using.
 .
 Both the iptables TPROXY targets and the nft compat layer are declared
 because this firmware's iptables is the nft-backed wrapper: how the target
 is expressed differs, and which kernel module backs it depends on that.
 .
 Both IPv4 and IPv6 are captured; IPv6 can be disabled with
 `uci set homeproxy.global.ipv6=0`.
 .
 Two properties of the target firmware are handled explicitly rather than
 assumed away. Its eqos service flushes the entire mangle PREROUTING chain on
 every interface event, so a resident guard watches the rules and re-installs
 them when it finds them gone. And its dns_redirect sends every UDP port-53
 query to the router's own dnsmasq; the rules step aside for that instead of
 competing with it, because a second handler for the same query leaves the
 reply outside what conntrack recorded and the client waits forever.
 .
 The guard is also what keeps the rules off a dead proxy. TPROXY rules in
 front of a port nobody is listening on are worse than no rules at all: the
 packet is dropped with no RST and no ICMP, so the LAN loses every foreign
 destination instead of falling back to the ordinary route. The guard pulls
 the rules after 20 seconds without a listener, puts them back when it
 returns, and -- because procd gives up after five crashes -- asks for a
 restart itself, at most three times per healthy period.
 .
 Three defects the frontend had are fixed in this build. The DNS strategy
 dropdown never reached the generated config: the generator read an option
 named `dns_strategy` that exists nowhere, so every choice fell through to
 the built-in default. A node name containing a space, a `#` or non-ASCII
 characters -- which includes the name the importer gives a duplicate --
 was rejected by two controller whitelists, leaving the node unselectable
 and untestable; both now accept what the page actually sends, and the
 status page escapes node names and connection hosts on the way into
 innerHTML. And the node page's collapse, latency test and sorting were
 inert, because their selector looked for a `fieldset` where this
 firmware's CBI templates render a `div`.
 .
 r27 fixes a deadlock the r25 watch phase introduced. rc.common takes procd's
 per-service lock on fd 1000 in start() and expects it back when the process
 that took it exits -- which a resident guard never does. The lock was
 therefore held for the whole life of the service, and every later stop(),
 reload() and opkg prerm -- all of which call procd_lock and block on the
 second, blocking flock when the non-blocking attempt fails -- waited on it
 forever. It also made the guard's own recovery path unreachable, since the
 restart it asks for is exactly the call that deadlocked. The guard now closes
 fd 1000 at startup. Upgrading from r26 or earlier needs the running guard
 killed by hand first, or this very bug will hang the prerm.
 .
 r28 is the result of an audit of the package against the running system.
 Eight further defects, each confirmed against the code and the device:
 .
 The guard's acquire conditions were latched -- once the port had been seen
 listening it was never tested again -- so a listener that died before the WAN
 came up still authorised the install, putting the full capture in front of a
 closed port and logging that the proxy was listening. They are re-read every
 tick now. The hotplug hook gated on `pidof sing-box`, which is true for the
 whole of a stop window (stop_service removes guard.pid and then runs
 firewall.sh down, and rc.common does not kill the service until it returns),
 so an interface event in that window re-installed the rules in front of a
 proxy being stopped, and for a plain stop nothing ever removed them again;
 it now tests the tproxy port, as the guard does. And stop_guard collected the
 guard's children before signalling it, so an `up` forked in that gap survived
 and rebuilt the rules after `down`; the guard is SIGSTOPped across the
 collection now.
 .
 The rule-set refresher stamped its throttle unconditionally, so a run in
 which every mirror failed -- the six-hourly cron during a reboot, or the
 postinst fetch with no WAN -- suppressed the next attempt for a week, which
 is also the only automatic repair path for a missing or truncated file. It
 stamps only on success. Its downloads shared one fixed temp path with no
 lock, so two overlapping runs could install a byte-mix of two downloads that
 passes the size and magic check and then fails inside sing-box; the temp name
 is per-process and the script takes a lock. And it restarted the whole service
 on every changed rule set, which it need not: verified on the device that
 sing-box watches the rule-set directory and reloads on the rename the script
 performs.
 .
 confgen now guards the two places where unvalidated input could stop the core
 from starting. Its duplicate-tag disambiguation never covered the generator's
 own tags, so a node named `direct`, `block` or `proxy` produced a duplicate
 outbound and sing-box exited during construction -- one node, not two. And
 rule_set_interval is free text from a CBI field with no datatype, emitted into
 a `type: remote` fallback that exists to make startup more robust; an
 unparseable value there fails config decode, so it is validated now.
 .
 The Update buttons on the subscription page ran the whole fetch synchronously
 inside the LuCI request, so a stalled or black-holed provider URL produced a
 502 from uhttpd's 60s script_timeout while the fetch ran on unwatched as a
 grandchild; they start it detached and the page's existing log pane reports
 progress. subscribe.lua itself took no lock, so two overlapping runs could
 lose the nodes one of them had just imported while still advancing that
 subscription's last_update and suppressing the next attempt; it takes one now.
 A hysteria2 node typed in by hand had its Obfs Password saved and then
 dropped, because the generator required an obfs_type that only the link parser
 ever sets.
 .
 r29 fixes a doubled tab strip. The package draws its own child-page navigation
 because the vendor theme renders none, but some themes render one, and this
 device runs one of those: argon ships an empty <div id="tabmenu"> in its header
 and fills it from the menu tree in JavaScript, so every homeproxy page showed
 that strip with this package's own stacked underneath -- five entries above,
 four below. The strip now stands down whenever the active theme draws its own.
 The test reads the theme's header, which is where both mechanisms are anchored:
 a server-side theme loops over node_childs there, and a client-side one puts
 the container there for its renderer to find. When the theme cannot be
 identified it still renders -- a missing strip leaves the five pages reachable
 only by typing URLs, while a duplicate is merely ugly, so that is the direction
 to be wrong in.
 .
 r30 makes the node page's collapsed line say what it collapsed. Each node was
 already folded to one summary row, but that row never showed a node's name,
 because CBI does not put the value on the element it gives the id to: the id
 goes on a wrapper div -- `<div id="cbid.homeproxy.<section>.name">` -- and
 /luci-static/resources/cbi.js builds the real control *inside* it at runtime.
 Reading `.value` off the id'd element read it off a div, where it is
 undefined, so every node summarised as "(unnamed)" with no address -- which
 is what the feature not being there at all looks like. The summary descends
 to the control now, shows the node's type beside its name, and is rewritten
 once those controls exist: cbi.js builds them only after luci.js has finished
 a probe of the RPC endpoint, a network round trip after DOMContentLoaded, so
 at the moment the row is created there is genuinely nothing on the page to
 read. It is rewritten on edits too, through a delegated listener, instead of
 leaving the old name behind.
 .
 Two smaller defects in the same file. Sorting moved the node bodies but not
 their Delete buttons, because tsection.htm emits a section's Delete in a
 sibling div *before* it: after a TCP sweep reordered the list, every Delete
 button sat next to the wrong node, and pressing it would have removed the
 wrong server. The pair moves together now, and the sorted list is inserted
 before the section's Add button rather than past it. And the whole list opens
 or closes from one button that always offers whichever action the list is not
 already in.
 .
 r31 adds `self_proxy`: capturing the traffic the *router itself* originates,
 not only what the LAN forwards. Measured on the device before the change,
 every rule lived in `mangle PREROUTING` behind `-i br-lan`, and both OUTPUT
 chains were empty -- so the router's own DNS, its NTP and the vendor's cloud
 bridge all left unproxied, and no amount of "global mode" could have caught
 them.
 .
 The kernel decides the shape of the fix: xt_TPROXY registers for PREROUTING
 and INPUT only, so `-j TPROXY` in OUTPUT is not a rule that fails at runtime,
 it is one iptables refuses to accept. What works -- and what this uses -- is
 what the firmware's own PassWall does: mark the first packet of each new
 connection in mangle OUTPUT, let the `fwmark 480 -> table 120 -> local dev lo`
 pair that the forwarded half already installs hand the packet back over the
 loopback interface, and catch it in a second PREROUTING jump matching
 `-i lo`, where TPROXY is legal. Every TCP and UDP packet is marked, with no
 CONNMARK: this firmware's ip6tables has no CONNMARK extension at all, so the
 usual mark-the-SYN-and-restore design would install cleanly on IPv4, be
 refused on IPv6, and leave the v6 half marking only the first packet of each
 flow -- a connection that opens and then stalls. Marking everything needs
 nothing from conntrack and is what the forwarded half already does. Verified
 end to end on the target device: with only these rules active the router's own
 `curl https://api.ipify.org` returned the node's address.
 .
 The failure this had to be designed against is a loop. sing-box runs as root,
 so `-m owner` cannot tell it apart from anything else, and if its own
 connection to the node were captured it would be handed back to itself and
 re-dialled at line rate. The discriminator is therefore SO_MARK: the generator
 now puts `routing_mark` on every outbound that dials, not just on `direct` as
 before, and the OUTPUT chain RETURNs on that mark before it does anything
 else -- first rule in the chain, because CONNMARK restore would otherwise
 overwrite it. A `routing_mark` of 0 is refused rather than obeyed, since
 sing-box reads it as "no mark" and the exclusion would match nothing.
 .
 DNS (53 and 853), NTP (123), the router's own addresses and the private
 ranges are left direct, unconditionally, on both families. DNS because
 sing-box resolves the node's hostname through 223.5.5.5 -- putting the
 resolver behind the tunnel it is needed to reach is a loop by another name;
 NTP because clock skew breaks the TLS the tunnel is made of. ICMP is left
 alone too: only TCP SYNs and `udp ctstate NEW` are marked, so ping, PMTU
 discovery and NDP keep working. The switch is off by default and one
 `uci set` away. SSH into the box cannot be cut off by a mistake here: that
 session is inbound, so it never traverses the OUTPUT chain, and its replies
 are addressed to the LAN, which the returns above cover.
 .
 It also fixes a latent bug found while wiring that up. `UCI="uci -q get
 homeproxy"` followed by `$UCI global.ipv6` splits on the spaces into two
 arguments, and this uci rejects `uci get <config> <path>` with a usage
 message -- so *every* option read in firewall.sh had been failing and taking
 its `|| echo <default>` branch. That went unnoticed only because each default
 happens to match this device: 5330, br-lan, ipv6 1, dns_hijack 1,
 routing_mark 2. The settings were inert rather than wrong, and changing
 tproxy_port in LuCI would have desynchronised the firewall from the generated
 sing-box config in silence. Reads now go through a helper that passes one
 argument. The state file `check` consults is new for the same reason: it
 records what `up` actually managed to install, so a half that legitimately
 could not be built does not make `check` fail forever and send the guard into
 a reinstall every ten seconds.
